description:
Root cause found while hardening frankensim-ext-program-risk-register-ppvs: fs-ledger::Ledger::link unconditionally inserts an edge and does not require the target op to be unfinished. A caller holding the same mutable Ledger can therefore add an edge after finish_op, changing lineage semantics behind an already-published terminal receipt. Program-risk replay/recovery now detects the changed exact edge set and fails closed, but that is a recovery-DoS boundary, not immutable provenance; there is also a verify-to-terminal TOCTOU while lineage and terminal publication use separate transactions.

Implement the invariant ledger-wide rather than special-casing fs-session:
- link must succeed only while the op outcome is NULL and must atomically distinguish missing, already-finished, and corrupt ops;
- finish_op must remain the one sealing transition;
- prove link-after-finish refusal, concurrent link/finish ordering, transaction rollback, branch/commit/explain compatibility, and no regression for existing producers;
- audit any code that intentionally links after finish and redesign its ordering;
- document whether the ops/edges schema itself enforces the invariant or the API is the sole authority;
- add a retained G0/G3/G4 regression and run the configured DSR/RCH proof lane.

Acceptance: after finish_op returns Ok, no public fs-ledger API can add, remove, or role-shift an edge for that op; explain/commit leaves remain stable; a concurrent or retried late link returns a typed refusal and changes zero rows.

## Background and why this is P0

The live workspace has two incompatible manifold semantics. fs-opt::eval::Manifold is the admitted production model: Rn and Sphere use ambient parameters, So3 uses a three-coordinate Lie-algebra increment followed by quaternion exponential, and Stiefel uses a QR retraction. fs-ascent::riemann and runner::Packing still carry an older execution model: embedded four-coordinate So3, no Stiefel support, locally duplicated projection/retraction rules, and no single typed authority for parameter dimension, point storage dimension, curve derivative, vector transport, or state replay. An older fs-opt/src/manifold.rs module is orphaned and must not become a third authority.

This split can produce a problem that passes fs-opt admission but changes meaning or is refused when executed by fs-ascent. It also makes theorem claims about Riemannian convergence, Wolfe curvature, transported L-BFGS secants, KKT residuals, and deterministic resume dependent on which copy of the manifold code happened to run. The defect is architectural, not a compatibility wrinkle.

## Mission

Create one authoritative, typed manifold-operation contract used by fs-opt admission, fs-ascent solvers, and runner Packing. Preserve the strongest currently implemented behavior, add the missing mathematical obligations, and make every unsupported or unproved case fail closed with structured no-claim diagnostics. Do not retain duplicate policy behind wrappers.

## Required design

1. Choose an ownership location that obeys the layer DAG and cannot create an L4 cycle. Define explicit PointDim, ParamDim, TangentDim, and representation semantics; point storage size and optimization-coordinate size must never be inferred to be equal.
2. Expose typed validation for manifold descriptors, points, parameter vectors, ambient gradients, tangent vectors, retraction inputs, and persisted solver state. Validation must happen before any user callback or budget-consuming evaluation.
3. Implement one canonical Rn path and one canonical sphere path. Sphere behavior must specify normalization, near-antipodal handling, tangent projection, geodesic or named retraction curve, curve velocity for line-search derivatives, and deterministic vector transport.
4. Implement So(3) with a three-coordinate Lie-algebra increment, stable small-angle quaternion exponential, explicit quaternion normalization, deterministic antipodal sign canonicalization, tangent/parameter mapping, retraction curve derivative, and transport consistent with the declared metric. Embedded-S3 behavior may remain only as an explicitly different manifold type with a distinct identity and contract.
5. Implement Stiefel tangent projection and a deterministic QR or polar retraction with sign-canonical factorization. Specify the metric, differentiated retraction curve, transport, singular/rank-deficient behavior, and exact dimensions for St(n,p).
6. Make the Riemannian solver consume the authoritative operations. Strong-Wolfe directional derivatives must follow the actual retraction curve. Every stored L-BFGS secant must live in the current tangent space; transport old pairs after every accepted iterate, recompute curvature/rho after non-isometric transport, and drop pairs that cannot meet finite relative-curvature obligations.
7. Make runner Packing use exactly the same operations without adding solver-only tangent preconditions to ambient basis probes used for packing Jacobians.
8. Honor hard evaluation budgets exactly, including zero. Problem construction/admission must be lazy or separately budgeted so a zero-evaluation solve cannot spend one hidden objective/gradient call. Cancellation is request, drain, finalize at declared boundaries and must leave no partially accepted iterate or pair.
9. Seal mutable solver state behind validated constructors or private fields. Define a versioned canonical state digest that binds manifold identity and dimensions, point bits, gradient bits, iteration/evaluation counters, stopping reason, line-search state, and every private curvature-pair bit needed for deterministic continuation.
10. Define structured typed errors for dimension mismatch, non-finite values, non-unit or rank-deficient points, singular retractions, incompatible resumes, exhausted budget, cancellation, and unsupported theorem obligations. No panic, NaN propagation, silent normalization of invalid state, or false convergence is allowed after a problem has been admitted.

## Mathematical and theorem ratchets

Keep the ambitious path open. Behind [M] feature gates, pursue executable local and eventually global theorems for transport isometry, metric compatibility, differentiated retractions, injectivity/convexity neighborhoods, line-search existence, and convergence rates on supported compact manifolds. The default contract may claim only what its certificates prove, but this issue must not weaken an ambitious target merely because the proof is not yet known. A theorem artifact should name assumptions, domain, numerical enclosure method where applicable, proof/checker identity, counterexample boundary, and exact solver capability it unlocks.

Investigate a sheaf-compatible manifold-operation atlas: local chart witnesses for retraction/transport/metric obligations glue into a global capability certificate only when overlap cocycles agree. Use this to distinguish globally certified behavior from locally valid chart behavior and to make topology-changing or multi-chart optimization fail closed at incompatible overlaps.

## Gauntlet and acceptance evidence

G0 unit/property laws:
- exact dimension and finiteness rejection occurs before callbacks;
- projection is tangent and idempotent within a justified bound;
- retraction returns a valid point or a typed failure;
- zero step is identity; small-step derivative matches the declared tangent direction;
- transport lands tangent; norm and inner-product preservation are tested whenever claimed;
- quaternion sign equivalents produce one canonical identity;
- Stiefel orthonormality and factor-sign canonicalization hold;
- malformed, NaN, infinite, zero-norm, antipodal, and rank-deficient cases fail as contracted;
- zero memory stores no pair and zero evaluation budget invokes no callback.

G1/G2:
- manufactured sphere, So3, and Stiefel objectives recover known optima and measured local orders;
- independent finite-difference or complex-step directional checks validate retraction-curve derivatives;
- compare against isolated development-only reference oracles where useful, never as production dependencies.

G3 metamorphic:
- orthogonal basis changes, quaternion sign flips, rigid rotations, unit/scaling changes, equivalent chart choices, and split-run resume preserve the specified result or transform it covariantly;
- independent intrinsic residual and KKT oracles are recomputed from final points rather than copied from production reports;
- adversarial non-isometric transports exercise rho recomputation and pair rejection.

G4:
- exact budgets at 0, 1, line-search boundary, and nested budget leaves;
- cancellation and injected callback faults during search/zoom/transport drain cleanly and commit no partial state;
- malformed resumed states and digest/version mismatches fail before callbacks;
- bounded cancellation latency and no task/arena leak.

G5:
- bit-stable fixed-ISA receipts across thread counts;
- one-shot and pause/serialize/resume/fork runs have identical canonical state and result digests;
- frozen reference receipts bind independent residuals, capability/no-claim status, solver configuration, manifold identity, and all private continuation state;
- seeded red mutations to result, budget, transport, sign canonicalization, and resume state are rejected.

## Completion criteria

All admitted manifold variants execute through the one authority; fs-opt and fs-ascent no longer disagree about So3 or Stiefel; duplicate/orphan authority is retired only with explicit user-approved deletion or otherwise made unreachable and clearly deprecated without deleting files; contracts and generated catalogs agree; relevant unit, conformance, replay, cancellation, determinism, and Clippy lanes are green through the configured central DSR/RCH proof process. Record exact commands, frozen receipt identities, and remaining no-claim boundaries. Do not close on compilation or a self-consistency test alone.

PHASE EPIC. Part of the new-domains expansion program (source: COMPREHENSIVE_PLAN_TO_EXTEND_FRANKENSIM_TO_NEW_DOMAINS.md, commit 83a0b49 — but this bead graph is self-contained; do not need the doc).

WHY THIS PHASE EXISTS: Every downstream domain (thermochemistry, EM, machine graphs, evidence receipts) needs schema-stable foundations that are catastrophically expensive to change later: the six-base unit system (amount of substance), stable machine identity (BodyId/SurfacePatchId/ContactFeatureId/StateSlotId with lineage), the versioned L6 machine-graph IR, and versioned ledger/package receipt migrations. Doing these FIRST is the cheapest moment in program history: only ~50 open beads and no new-domain consumers exist yet.

SCOPE: six-base/semantic units (ext-qty-six-base); machine IR + stable identity/lineage (ext-machine-ir); ledger/package receipt/checkpoint migration (ext-ledger-package-migration); E0a test battery (ext-e0a-test-battery).

EXIT CRITERIA (all Gauntlet-gated; every exit item must expand to an executable gate naming input deck, QoI, acceptance band, evidence class, scale/hardware, budgets, cancellation bound, determinism class, and fallback — broad prose alone cannot close the phase):
1. five-to-six wire decoding works and old/new content hashes are BOTH immutable (old_hash→new_hash crosswalk recorded, never overwritten);
2. all literal/generic dimension-vector consumers migrated (fs-regime Buckingham-π, fs-opt, fs-ir, fs-scenario::ir, fs-ledger, fs-package, fs-crosswalk, parsers, JSON, goldens, plus the standalone fs-wasm workspace);
3. FrankenScript → scenario → package stable-ID/hash round trip passes;
4. ambiguous lineage REFUSES (typed error) instead of silently rebinding.

PARALLELISM: E0a/E0b/E0c can run concurrently; E0d needs all three. Nothing in E1+ may merge against unmigrated five-base schemas.

LATENT PANIC (low-probability, robustness) in crates/fs-race/src/lib.rs (~line 391), found in the deep-review hunt. Analyzed rigorously; deliberately NOT landed because I could not build a deterministic regression test (see reachability analysis).

## The path
`race_field`'s round loop condemns candidates via `e_benjamini_hochberg(&log_e, alpha)` (no "keep at least one" constraint), setting `alive[i]=false` for every returned index. After the loop, `survivors = (0..n).filter(alive)` and `winner = survivors.iter().min_by(...).expect("finite race inputs leave at least one survivor")`. If a round condemns EVERY live candidate, `survivors` is empty and the `.expect()` PANICS — a crash on an unlucky/adversarial field, which is worse than a graceful result for an anytime-valid racer.

## Reachability (corrects an overconfident first-pass claim)
A naive "rock-paper-scissors loss field condemns all three" trigger does NOT work: with a scalar loss and the linear pairwise normalization d_ji = ((loss_i - loss_j)/span + 1)/2, the three cyclic d-values sum to EXACTLY 1.5 every round (telescoping), so the cyclic "j beats i" e-processes cannot all grow. More generally the e-relation "j strongly beats i" holds iff mean(loss_j) < mean(loss_i) — a TOTAL ORDER by mean loss — so the min-mean-loss candidate is beaten by no one and ASYMPTOTICALLY always survives (the `.expect()` holds w.h.p.). The panic is only reachable via an improbable FINITE-SAMPLE e-process spike (Ville: any single e-process exceeds T with prob <= 1/T; over many rounds x many pairs a union-bound spike that simultaneously inflates EVERY candidate's mixture past m/alpha is possible but astronomically rare). I could not force it with a deterministic loss field (verified empirically: a strong period-3 intransitive field eliminated no one in 400 rounds, exactly as the sum=1.5 constraint predicts).

## Proposed fix (prototyped, reverted)
Before applying the condemnation, if `condemned.len() == live.len() && !live.is_empty()`, spare the STRONGEST survivor — the one with the smallest mixture log-e, tie-break lowest index — so at least one always survives:
```
let mut condemned = e_benjamini_hochberg(&log_e, settings.alpha);
if condemned.len() == live.len() && !live.is_empty() {
    let spare = (0..live.len())
        .min_by(|&a, &b| log_e[a].total_cmp(&log_e[b]).then(live[a].cmp(&live[b])))
        .expect("live is non-empty");
    condemned.retain(|&k| k != spare);
}
```
This is behavior-preserving for every existing test (the guard only fires when a round would empty the field) and converts the rare panic into a graceful spare. The full battery (13 tests) passed with it applied.

## Why beaded, not landed
Verify-before-fix discipline: a fix whose triggering path I cannot demonstrate deterministically shouldn't land unproven. The maintainers should decide whether to (a) land the defensive guard (a crash in an anytime racer is a real reliability risk, even if rare), (b) formally prove the `.expect()` invariant and keep it as-is, or (c) return a dedicated `RaceError::NoSurvivor` instead of sparing. If landing (a) or (c), note that a deterministic regression test likely requires either injecting e-values directly (not currently exposed) or a very long adversarial run tuned to force a correlated spike.

